{
  "message": "**Question:** When a tier's quota and bucket capacity differ, should `limit` and `X-RateLimit-Limit` report the quota or the capacity?\nWhy it matters: Callers and acceptance tests need a consistent definition of the advertised limit.\n\n**Recommended:** Option A - Report the configured per-minute quota, since `limit` describes the rate policy, while capacity controls burst allowance.\n\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| A | Report quota in `limit` and `X-RateLimit-Limit` |\n| B | Report bucket capacity in both |\n| Short | Provide another short answer (<=5 words) |\n\nYou can reply with the option letter (e.g., \"A\"), accept the recommendation by saying \"yes\" or \"recommended\", or provide your own short answer.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "decision": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Reported limit",
        "description": "Choose quota or capacity; you may provide another answer in <=5 words.",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "quota",
            "title": "A — Quota (recommended)"
          },
          {
            "const": "capacity",
            "title": "B — Capacity"
          }
        ],
        "default": "quota"
      }
    }
  }
}

{
  "message": "**Question:** If the same `client_id` is submitted with a different tier, should it use a separate bucket, switch policy on the same bucket, or be rejected?\nWhy it matters: The identity rule determines whether a caller can get additional quota by changing the tier value.\n\n**Recommended:** Option C - Keep one bucket per `client_id`, bind it to the tier on first valid use, and reject a later tier mismatch with HTTP 400. This avoids separate quota pools and prevents silently switching policies for an existing bucket.\n\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| A | Bucket identity is (`client_id`, `tier`); each tier has separate state |\n| B | Bucket identity is `client_id`; each request uses its submitted tier's settings |\n| C | Bucket identity is `client_id`; bind to the first tier and reject mismatches with HTTP 400 |\n| Short | Provide another short answer (<=5 words) |\n\nYou can reply with the option letter (e.g., \"C\"), accept the recommendation by saying \"yes\" or \"recommended\", or provide your own short answer.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "decision": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Bucket identity and tier changes",
        "description": "Choose A, B, or C; you may provide another answer in <=5 words.",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "pair",
            "title": "A — Separate bucket per client and tier"
          },
          {
            "const": "client-switch",
            "title": "B — One bucket, tier policy may change"
          },
          {
            "const": "client-bound",
            "title": "C — One bucket, bind first tier and reject mismatches (recommended)"
          }
        ],
        "default": "client-bound"
      }
    }
  }
}

{
  "message": "Which bucket behavior do you choose for this same question: A (separate bucket for each `client_id` and tier), B (one client bucket that uses the submitted tier's settings), or C (one client bucket bound to its first tier, with later tier mismatches rejected with HTTP 400)? You can also answer in <=5 words.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "decision": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Choose A, B, or C",
        "enum": [
          "A",
          "B",
          "C"
        ],
        "default": "C"
      }
    }
  }
}

{
  "message": "**Question:** When one request has several validation problems, in what order should the service choose the error response?\nWhy it matters: A defined order makes error behavior predictable and lets tests assert one exact response for multiply-invalid requests.\n\n**Recommended:** Option A - Validate malformed JSON, then require a JSON object, then validate `client_id`, then validate `tier`. This follows the structural-to-field order you identified and avoids checking fields before they can be read safely.\n\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| A | Valid JSON → JSON object → `client_id` → `tier` |\n| B | Valid JSON → JSON object → `tier` → `client_id` |\n| C | Return errors in an implementation-defined order |\n| Short | Provide another short answer (<=5 words) |\n\nYou can reply with the option letter (e.g., \"A\"), accept the recommendation by saying \"yes\" or \"recommended\", or provide your own short answer.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "decision": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Validation order",
        "description": "Choose A, B, or C; you may provide another answer in <=5 words.",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "json-object-client-tier",
            "title": "A — JSON, object, client_id, tier (recommended)"
          },
          {
            "const": "json-object-tier-client",
            "title": "B — JSON, object, tier, client_id"
          },
          {
            "const": "implementation-defined",
            "title": "C — Implementation-defined"
          }
        ],
        "default": "json-object-client-tier"
      }
    }
  }
}

{
  "message": "**Question:** Should HTTP 200 responses include `X-RateLimit-Limit` and `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and should `Retry-After` appear only on HTTP 429 responses?\nWhy it matters: Clients need to know whether the header contract is uniform for successful checks and when retry guidance is meaningful.\n\n**Recommended:** Option A - Include both rate-limit headers on HTTP 200 with values matching the response body, and send `Retry-After` only on HTTP 429. The retry delay applies only when a request is actually rejected.\n\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| A | 200 includes both `X-RateLimit-*` headers; `Retry-After` only on 429 |\n| B | No `X-RateLimit-*` headers on 200; `Retry-After` only on 429 |\n| C | 200 includes both rate-limit headers and `Retry-After` |\n| Short | Provide another short answer (<=5 words) |\n\nYou can reply with the option letter (e.g., \"A\"), accept the recommendation by saying \"yes\" or \"recommended\", or provide your own short answer.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "decision": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Response headers",
        "description": "Choose A, B, or C; you may provide another answer in <=5 words.",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "success-rate-headers-retry-429-only",
            "title": "A — Rate headers on 200; retry header only on 429 (recommended)"
          },
          {
            "const": "retry-429-only",
            "title": "B — No rate headers on 200; retry header only on 429"
          },
          {
            "const": "retry-both",
            "title": "C — Rate headers and retry on 200"
          }
        ],
        "default": "success-rate-headers-retry-429-only"
      }
    }
  }
}

{
  "message": "**Question:** Which policy should the service use for invalid tier settings and a clock that moves backwards?\nWhy it matters: Invalid numeric values can break refill/retry calculations, while backward time can corrupt token state or make outcomes nondeterministic.\n\n**Recommended:** Option A - Require quota and capacity to be positive numeric values, rejecting zero, negative, and non-numeric settings; treat backward clock movement as zero elapsed time and retain the last recorded time. This keeps refill math defined and prevents backward time from creating tokens.\n\n| Option | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| A | Require positive numeric quota/capacity; clamp backward time to zero elapsed |\n| B | Require positive numeric quota/capacity; reset bucket state on backward time |\n| C | Permit zero values; clamp backward time to zero elapsed |\n| D | Provide another short answer (<=5 words) |\n\nYou can reply with the option letter (e.g., \"A\"), accept the recommendation by saying \"yes\" or \"recommended\", or provide your own short answer.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "decision": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Configuration and clock policy",
        "description": "Choose A, B, or C; you may provide another answer in <=5 words.",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "positive-clamp",
            "title": "A — Positive numeric values; backward time adds zero elapsed (recommended)"
          },
          {
            "const": "positive-reset",
            "title": "B — Positive numeric values; reset bucket when time goes backward"
          },
          {
            "const": "zero-allowed-clamp",
            "title": "C — Zero allowed; backward time adds zero elapsed"
          }
        ],
        "default": "positive-clamp"
      }
    }
  }
}
